==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|wauld}} {{q|Scotland}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/wɔːld/}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|walden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wealdan||to rule, control, determine, direct, command, govern, possess, wield, exercise, cause, bring about}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*waldan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*waldaną||to reign}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*waldʰ-||to be strong, be powerful, prevail, possess}}. 

{{rel-top|Cognates}}
Cognate with {{cog|de|walten||to prevail, reign, dominate}}, {{cog|da|-}} {{m|sv|volde||to cause}}, {{cog|is|valda||to cause}}, {{cog|lt|valda||land property}}, {{cog|lt|valdyti||to rule}}. {{rel-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal|ambitransitive}} To [[govern]]; [[inherit]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|wald}}, {{m|enm|iwald}}, from {{inh|en|ang|weald||high land covered with wood, woods, forest}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*waldą||power}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*waldʰ-||to be strong, be powerful, prevail, possess}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|Gewalt||force, power, control, violence}}, {{cog|sv|våld||force, violence}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal}} [[power|Power]]; [[strength]].
# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal}} [[command|Command]]; [[control]]; [[possession]].

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|wield}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm||wald|}}, from {{inh|en|ang|weald||high land covered with wood, woods, forest}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*walþu}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*walþuz}}, whence also Old High German {{m|goh|wald}} (German {{m|de|Wald}}) and Old Norse {{m|non|vǫllr}} (Faroese {{m|fo|vøllur}}, Norwegian {{m|no|voll}}, Icelandic {{m|is|völlur}}).

